WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.047 --> 00:00:06.147
Scraps records edible food waste. Every input in this walkthrough is fictional, not a household trial.

2
00:00:06.147 --> 00:00:12.286
The separate example totals 2.03 kg. Its emissions and water figures use global production averages.

3
00:00:12.286 --> 00:00:18.371
Beef leads by estimated emissions; milk leads by weight. The example does not overwrite the personal log.

4
00:00:18.371 --> 00:00:25.915
Two fictional entries: 1,000 g of bread in the previous week and 100 g of beef in the latest week.

5
00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:32.419
An unchecked day means missing data, not zero waste. Thirteen completed days are not enough for comparison.

6
00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:42.496
After all 14 days are complete: 90% less weight, but 8.38 kg CO2e higher estimated production emissions.

7
00:00:42.496 --> 00:00:50.614
Food-level changes explain the total: beef adds 9.95 kg CO2e, while bread falls by 1.57 kg CO2e. Values are rounded.

8
00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:58.689
Water tells a different story: bread falls by 648 L and beef rises by 145.1 L, for a net 502.9 L decrease.

9
00:00:58.689 --> 00:01:06.794
The two entries and completion marks survive a reload. These are changes in logs, not proven environmental savings.

10
00:01:06.794 --> 00:01:15.901
Simulated damage: the recording script inserts an invalid saved row, then reloads. Scraps warns and reopens its day.

11
00:01:15.901 --> 00:01:24.969
The comparison stays unavailable until the log is checked and completed again. This video verifies app behaviour, not household impact.
